    fix(land-node-branch): run the gate on the already-landed path too (CF-303)
    
    The already-landed (idempotence) path reported success without running --gate-cmd.
    `scripts/land-node-branch.sh` measured the node's tree only on the merge path; when the
    envelope's commit was already an ancestor of <remote>/<main> it printed
    `LANDED (already): <branch> @ <commit> in <remote>/<main>` and exited 0 without ever
    creating the detached worktree the gate is supposed to measure. A commit that landed by
    another route, or landed and was broken afterwards, was therefore reported as landed-clean
    by the one check that would have caught it.
    
    Measured pre-fix on a scratch bare origin: landing the node branch once with a green gate,
    then running
    
      scripts/land-node-branch.sh --workdir <wd> --branch node/crier/fixture \
          --commit <sha already in origin/main> --gate-cmd 'exit 99'
    
    printed `LANDED (already): ...` and exited 0 — the gate never ran.
    
    Fix: the gate block is hoisted verbatim into `run_gate()` (detached worktree at $commit_sha,
    `bash -c "$GATE"` there, `PASS gate` on 0, last 5 log lines + `refuse gate` on nonzero,
    `cleanup` on both paths) and is called from BOTH success paths — the already-landed path
    before the LANDED line, and the existing place in the merge path. Refusal precedence is
    unchanged: the gate call stays below the `descendant` check, so a branch that needs a rebase
    is still refused as `descendant` first (probed: stale branch + `--gate-cmd 'exit 99'` refuses
    `descendant`, no gate row). --dry-run still runs no gate and creates no worktree; the
    already-landed dry-run path now prints one named row,
    `note gate "dry-run: not run — a gate worktree would be created at <sha12>"`. Argument
    parser, refusal vocabulary, push refspec, the no_ff_local guard and the post-push `landed`
    proof are untouched, and no worktree leaks on either path.
    
    Verification: tests/selftest.sh section 42, arm i rewritten into two arms — i1
    (`already-landed red gate`) asserts exit 1 with the named row
    `FAIL gate — 'echo fixture gate refused; exit 99' exited 99 at <node2>`, no `LANDED (already)`
    line and an unmoved HEAD (the pre-fix script exits 0 here, so the arm is red before the fix);
    i2 (`idempotent re-run`) asserts exit 0, `PASS gate —`, `LANDED (already)`, and both local
    HEAD and the bare origin's refs/heads/main unchanged. `bash -n scripts/land-node-branch.sh &&
    bash tests/selftest.sh` ends `SELFTEST: PASS` (exit 0, fails=0).
